<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

Every Energy card is a card with an optional title. The graph cards add a chart, a legend and, on some, a header button. The sources table is a real table. The gauges show one percentage. The distribution and Sankey cards draw diagrams of nodes joined by flow lines.

Every card accepts `title` and `collection_key`. Cards that share a `collection_key` use the period chosen on the [date selector](#child-hui-energy-date-selection-card).

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

**What the Civica theme sets.** The theme sets one colour for each energy role, in light and dark modes, using the Civica chart colours:

| Role | Theme key | Colour |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Grid consumption | `energy-grid-consumption-color` | #1D70B8 |
| Grid return | `energy-grid-return-color` | #0F385C |
| Solar | `energy-solar-color` | #F7996A |
| Gas | `energy-gas-color` | #CA357C |
| Water | `energy-water-color` | #50A1A5 |
| Non-fossil | `energy-non-fossil-color` | #50A1A5 |
| Battery in and out | `energy-battery-in-color`, `energy-battery-out-color` | #7F65B7 |
| Total consumption | `energy-consumption-color` | #0B0C0C in light mode, #FFFFFF in dark mode |

The role colours are the same in light and dark modes. Two roles, water and non-fossil, share a colour, so label them in words. These keys replace Home Assistant’s default energy colours. The card frame uses the theme’s card keys: square corners, a 1 pixel border and no shadow.

**What needs the Civica companion.** Chart geometry, 1.5 pixel power lines and the values table behind a chart come from the optional Civica companion, not from the theme. It covers six Energy graph cards and the power sources graph. The distribution diagram, the Sankey cards, the gauges, the grid balance card, the sources table, the compare notice and the date selector stay as Home Assistant draws them, and take only the theme keys.

**What needs card-mod.** Changes to the card frame beyond the theme keys need the card-mod integration.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-distribution-card"><code>hui-energy-distribution-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-distribution`. A diagram of how energy moves between the grid, solar panels, a battery, gas, water and the home. Extra setting: `link_dashboard`, which adds a button to the Energy dashboard. It takes the energy colours from the theme. The Civica companion does not restyle it. Read the numbers on the diagram out in words nearby when they matter, and see [Energy flow and other chart families](../home-assistant/energy-flow-and-other-charts.html).

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-sankey-card"><code>hui-energy-sankey-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-sankey`. A flow chart of the electricity used over the chosen period, in kWh. The grid, solar panels and battery supply the home, energy sent back to the grid and into the battery is shown, and the home’s use is split by device. Choosing a node opens that entity’s More info. Extra settings: `layout` (`auto`, `vertical` or `horizontal`), `group_by_floor` and `group_by_area` (both on by default), `max_devices` and `show_values`. With `auto`, the chart runs vertically on a small screen and horizontally otherwise. `max_devices` applies to each parent node, not to the whole chart: where a parent has more devices than the limit, the smallest are grouped into that parent’s “Other” node, and the limit is 20 unless you set one. Devices below 0.1% of the home’s total are grouped into “Other” as well. `show_values` shows the values on the chart and is off by default. The Civica companion does not restyle it, and it has no values table, so give the main values in words or in the [sources table](#child-hui-energy-sources-table-card).

<h3 id="child-hui-power-sankey-card"><code>hui-power-sankey-card</code></h3>

`type: power-sankey`. The same kind of flow chart for power, drawn from the current readings of the power sensors in the Energy configuration, in watts or kilowatts, not from the chosen period. It takes the same extra settings as [`hui-energy-sankey-card`](#child-hui-energy-sankey-card). With no power readings it says there is no data. Say in the card title that the values are the present moment.

<h3 id="child-hui-water-sankey-card"><code>hui-water-sankey-card</code></h3>

`type: water-sankey`. A flow chart of the water used over the chosen period, in the home’s water unit. Water from the supply is split by device. Choosing a node opens that entity’s More info. It takes the same extra settings as [`hui-energy-sankey-card`](#child-hui-energy-sankey-card). With no data for the period it says so. The Civica companion does not restyle it and it has no values table, so give the main values in words.

<h3 id="child-hui-water-flow-sankey-card"><code>hui-water-flow-sankey-card</code></h3>

`type: water-flow-sankey`. The same kind of flow chart for water flow right now, drawn from the current readings of the water flow-rate sensors in the Energy configuration, not from the chosen period. It takes the same extra settings as [`hui-energy-sankey-card`](#child-hui-energy-sankey-card). With no readings it says there is no data. Say in the card title that the values are the present moment.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-usage-graph-card"><code>hui-energy-usage-graph-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-usage-graph`. Energy used over the chosen period, as stacked columns. Extra settings: `show_legend` and `expand_legend`. The Civica companion restyles it.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-solar-graph-card"><code>hui-energy-solar-graph-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-solar-graph`. Solar energy produced over the period, with a forecast when a solar forecast is set up. No extra settings. The Civica companion restyles it.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-gas-graph-card"><code>hui-energy-gas-graph-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-gas-graph`. Gas used over the period. No extra settings. The Civica companion restyles it.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-water-graph-card"><code>hui-energy-water-graph-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-water-graph`. Water used over the period. No extra settings. The Civica companion restyles it.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-devices-graph-card"><code>hui-energy-devices-graph-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-devices-graph`. Energy used by individual devices, as bars or as a pie. Extra settings: `max_devices`, `hide_compound_stats`, `modes` (`bar` or `pie`) and `expand_legend`. A header button changes the view. The Civica companion restyles the bar view only; the pie stays as Home Assistant draws it.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-devices-detail-graph-card"><code>hui-energy-devices-detail-graph-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-devices-detail-graph`. Each device’s use over time. Extra settings: `max_devices` and `expand_legend`. The Civica companion restyles it.

<h3 id="child-hui-power-sources-graph-card"><code>hui-power-sources-graph-card</code></h3>

`type: power-sources-graph`. Power over the chosen period, as lines in kW for solar, grid, battery and the home’s consumption, drawn from the power sensors in the Energy configuration. The legend shows each source’s current power. Extra settings: `show_legend` and `expand_legend`. With no data for the period it says so. The Civica companion restyles it.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-sources-table-card"><code>hui-energy-sources-table-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-sources-table`. A table of energy sources with a label of “Energy sources”. Extra settings: `types`, which lists the source types to include, and `show_only_totals`. It is a real table, so its headers and rows keep their meaning. It takes its text and divider colours from the theme. It is also the best text version of the graph cards, so show it beside a graph when exact values matter.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-grid-neutrality-gauge-card"><code>hui-energy-grid-neutrality-gauge-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-grid-neutrality-gauge`. A gauge showing whether the home sent more energy to the grid than it took from it. Home Assistant explains the needle by colour, with a purple area for net export and a blue area for net import, in an information tooltip. It uses the grid return and grid consumption colours. No extra settings. Add a sentence that states the result in words, so the meaning never depends on the colour of the needle.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-grid-balance-card"><code>hui-energy-grid-balance-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-grid-balance`. A compact card with a title (“Grid energy balance” unless you set `title`) and a sum in kWh: energy imported from the grid, minus energy exported to it, equals the net. Under it a bar grows left for export and right for import, and marks the net on the side it falls. Each part has a sentence in a tooltip, such as “Net energy imported”. It uses the grid consumption and grid return colours. The sum already states the result, but say “net import” or “net export” in words nearby, so the meaning never depends on which side the bar grows.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-solar-consumed-gauge-card"><code>hui-energy-solar-consumed-gauge-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-solar-consumed-gauge`. A gauge showing how much of the solar energy produced the home used itself. No extra settings.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-carbon-consumed-gauge-card"><code>hui-energy-carbon-consumed-gauge-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-carbon-consumed-gauge`. A gauge showing how much of the electricity the home used was generated from non-fossil sources. No extra settings.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-self-sufficiency-gauge-card"><code>hui-energy-self-sufficiency-gauge-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-self-sufficiency-gauge`. A gauge showing how much of the electricity the home used came from on-site sources, such as solar panels or batteries. No extra settings.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-date-selection-card"><code>hui-energy-date-selection-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-date-selection`. Chooses the period that the other Energy cards show. Extra settings: `vertical_opening_direction` (`auto`, `up` or `down`), `opening_direction` (`auto`, `right`, `left`, `center` or `inline`) and `disable_compare`. Put it at the top of the view, above the cards it controls, and give every card the same `collection_key`.

<h3 id="child-hui-energy-compare-card"><code>hui-energy-compare-card</code></h3>

`type: energy-compare`. A notice that says which period is being compared with which. It has a button that switches between comparing with the previous period and with the previous year, and a close button that ends the comparison. It stays hidden while comparison is off, and in the card editor it shows a sample notice. It takes no extra settings. Put it directly under the [date selector](#child-hui-energy-date-selection-card), and give it the same `collection_key`.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The cards are native, so they follow the Energy configuration and the household’s units and costs.
- The theme gives one stable colour to each energy role in light and dark modes.
- Hover values on a graph need a text version. The Civica companion adds a values table to the graphs it covers; the gauges, the distribution diagram, the Sankey cards and the pie view have none, so state their result in words.
- Water and non-fossil share a colour in the theme.
- The theme alone cannot change chart shape, line width or legend layout.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Put the date selector first, then one idea per card. Show the unit and the period next to every value, such as “kWh this week”. Never infer a saving or a cost the card does not state. Name each source in words, because two roles share a colour. Where a value appears only on hover, give the same value in the sources table or in text.

See [Energy and history](../patterns/energy.html) for power and energy wording, and [Charts and tables](charts.html) for the table that sits behind a chart.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: give a chart a text version of its main values.
- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the sources table keeps its row and column headers.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: label each source in words; the legend and table carry the meaning.
- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: test each series against the card background in your own dashboard, in light and dark modes.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: test each card at 320 CSS pixels.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): test the date selector and the legend with a keyboard and by touch.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible: test focus on the date selector and on any header button.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: test that each gauge has a name and a value in your own setup.

